I once did the thought experiment and came up with the following plan:

(Disclaimer: I have very little experience with multi-lingual software
development so I may well be smoking crack^H^H^H^H^Hviolet mold here,
but it looks reasonable to me.)

    1) Translate all external messages and put them in different
    files.  Pick the one for the language we're using at startup.  (This
    is the easy part.)

    2) Go through the source code and mark up the generated parts of
    message strings to indicate which parts are generated at runtime.
    For example,
           "%s hits %s"
    becomes
           "<mon %s> hits <mon %s>"
    Some of this could be done by hacking up the existing
    string-generation functions.

    3) Translate each of the inlined strings into your target language
    and put the translations into a database.

    4) Write a translation routine which takes the message as input.
    Because of the markup, it knows that, when it gets
           "<mon The orc> hits <mon <name Kenny>>",
    it needs to look up "<> hits <>" and "The orc".  ("Kenny" is a
    proper name and so gets left alone.)  The replacement strings may
    contain their own markup.

    5) Write a routine which takes the results of 4) and, according to
    whatever markup remains, fixes up things like tense, case and noun
    gender.  This will almost certainly depend on the language itself.

    If there are constructions that are too difficult, go back to the
    translation strings and add more markup to provide hints.

    6) Just before the result is actually printed, strip out all of
    the markup.

Step #5 is going to be the trickiest part.  However, we can cheat by
giving it hints in markup so that it isn't an unsolved AI problem.

The nice part about this scheme is that it can be folded back into the
original sources (should the DevTeam accept it, that is) and so make
Nethack i18n-friendly.  Translations to other languages would become
relatively easy.  It would actually be possible to switch languages
while the game is running!

This could also be used to simplify the source code.  All of those
special cases where hallucination causes different messages could be
replaced with a single "alternate language" for hallucination.

Of course, it's a _huge_ amount of work for whoever volunteers to do
it.  Which may well be why nobody has.


